  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "R6 and R12 strongly point to adding Cr to a closely related refractory BCC alloy: Cr promotes Cr-rich Laves-phase precipitation, while Cr addition supplies solid-solution strengthening and the Laves phase supplies additional strengthening. R2 independently supports Laves precipitation inside a refractory BCC matrix, and R14-R16 show that appropriately arranged hard second phases and a continuous ductile matrix can combine strength with useful plasticity. However, none of the records describes a reticulated multicomponent Laves network, identifies such a brittle network as a crack-propagation barrier, or predicts that it will increase fracture strain. Several records instead associate Laves or brittle reinforcing phases with cracking and lost ductility. Thus, the literature points toward the Cr intervention and part of the strengthening mechanism, but the network-mediated plasticity enhancement requires a substantial additional idea."
